>
  <p>
    Gin Rummy is the classic two-player card game of building tidy combinations and getting out
    before your opponent. You draw and discard one card at a time, arranging your hand into
    <strong>melds</strong> and trimming the leftover <strong>deadwood</strong> until you can safely
    end the hand. This page is a complete reference to the rules. To learn by doing, you can
    <a href="/gin/">play Gin Rummy</a> right now against the computer.
  </p>

  <h2>What is Gin Rummy?</h2>
  <p>
    Gin Rummy is played by two people with a standard 52-card deck. Aces are <strong>low</strong>.
    The aim is to organise your hand into melds and reduce your unmatched cards to a low value, then
    end the hand on your terms. Hands are quick, and you play several of them, accumulating points
    until someone reaches the target of <strong>100</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>The deal</h2>
  <p>
    Each player is dealt <strong>10 cards</strong>. The next card is turned face up to start the
    <strong>discard pile</strong>, and the rest form the face-down <strong>stock</strong>. On each
    turn you draw one card and discard one, so your hand is always 10 cards at the end of your turn.
  </p>

  <h2>Melds: sets and runs</h2>
  <p>A meld is a valid group of cards. There are two kinds:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Set:</strong> three or four cards of the <strong>same rank</strong> — for example,
      three Sevens or four Queens.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Run:</strong> three or more cards of the <strong>same suit in sequence</strong> — for
      example, the 4-5-6 of hearts. Aces are low, so the lowest run is Ace-2-3; an ace does not wrap
      around to the King.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>A single card can only belong to one meld at a time, so part of the skill is choosing the best arrangement.</p>

  <h2>Deadwood</h2>
  <p>
    Any card left over that isn't in a meld is <strong>deadwood</strong>. Each deadwood card scores
    its pip value: number cards are face value, <strong>face cards are 10</strong>, and
    <strong>aces are 1</strong>. Add them up and you have your deadwood total — the number you are
    always trying to push down.
  </p>

  <h2>Your turn: draw and discard</h2>
  <p>On your turn you do two things in order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Draw</strong> one card — either the top of the face-down stock (unknown) or the top of
      the discard pile (known, but it tells your opponent what you're collecting).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Discard</strong> one card face up onto the discard pile.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Play alternates until someone ends the hand by knocking, or the stock runs too low to continue.
  </p>

  <h2>Knocking</h2>
  <p>
    When your deadwood total is <strong>10 or fewer</strong>, you may <strong>knock</strong> to end
    the hand (you knock as you make your final discard). You lay down your melds and your deadwood.
    Your opponent then lays down their melds and may <strong>lay off</strong> their own deadwood
    cards onto <em>your</em> melds — extending your run or completing your set — to shrink their own
    count. Then you compare deadwood: if yours is lower, you score the <strong>difference</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>Going gin</h2>
  <p>
    If you can meld <strong>all ten cards</strong> with <strong>zero deadwood</strong>, you go
    <strong>gin</strong>. This earns a <strong>25-point bonus</strong> on top of your opponent's
    deadwood, and crucially the opponent <strong>cannot lay off</strong> any cards. Going gin is
    always a clean, safe way to win a hand.
  </p>

  <h2>The undercut</h2>
  <p>
    Knocking has a risk. After lay-offs, if your opponent's deadwood is <strong>equal to or less
    than yours</strong>, they have <strong>undercut</strong> you. Instead of you scoring, they score
    the difference <strong>plus a 25-point undercut bonus</strong>. That is why you don't knock the
    instant you're able to — a thin knock can hand the hand to a well-set opponent.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring and winning</h2>
  <p>
    Points accumulate hand by hand: the knocker scores the deadwood difference, a gin adds 25, and an
    undercut gives the bonus to the defender. The first player to reach <strong>100 points</strong>
    wins the game. If the stock runs too low to continue (only two cards left) before anyone knocks,
    the hand is a wash and no one scores.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
